Selected idea
ChangeGuard AI
ChangeGuard AI takes a requirement and proposed pull request, identifies code outside the pull request that may break, converts the highest-risk failure hypothesis into an executable regression test, runs that test against the actual application, and determines whether the change is safe to merge.
If a valid test reproduces a regression, the release verdict becomes BLOCKED. IBM Bob proposes a minimal fix, but no source-code change is applied until a reviewer approves the exact stored diff. After approval, ChangeGuard applies only that reviewed patch, reruns the complete test suite and produces an evidence-backed Change Survival Report.
Core positioning
A green CI pipeline proves only the scenarios already covered by existing tests.
ChangeGuard uses IBM Bob to investigate what a change could break outside those existing tests and converts the risk into executable proof before merge.
Do not describe ChangeGuard as a generic:
AI code reviewer
AI test generator
PR summarizer
Static-analysis dashboard
Multi-agent SDLC assistant
Its differentiator is:
Change-specific, cross-component failure discovery followed by deterministic executable proof.
Primary demo scenario
Jira-style requirement PRICE-142:
“Support nullable promotional discount codes.”
The feature branch modifies the product contract from:
discountCode: string
to:
discountCode: string | null
The changed files are in the product/pricing area. An untouched billing component receives product data through a serialized event boundary and maintains an older copy of the contract:
discountCode: string
The billing calculator executes:
line.discountCode.toUpperCase()
Because the data crosses a JSON/event boundary and is cast to the billing type after JSON parsing, TypeScript compilation succeeds. The 18 existing tests also pass because:
Validator tests never send null.
Controller tests mock the billing adapter.
Billing tests use only non-null discount codes.
Billing does not import the modified product type.
ChangeGuard should:
Read the requirement and Git diff.
Detect the changed contract field.
Trace the event path to the untouched billing consumer.
Identify the null-dereference hypothesis.
Generate a real Jest regression test asserting that billing safely handles a null discount code.
Execute the test.
Confirm that it reached application code and failed at InvoiceTaxCalculator.
Mark the proof as valid.
Set the deterministic verdict to BLOCKED.
Generate the smallest safe patch.
Validate the diff with git apply --check and store its SHA-256.
Show the exact proposed diff to the reviewer.
Require explicit human approval.
Apply only the approved stored diff.
Rerun the complete suite.
Finish with 18 baseline tests plus one generated regression test passing.
Produce the Change Survival Report with evidence.
The primary demonstration should remain focused on this single contract regression.
Zero-price handling, authorization checks, security scanning, additional failure categories, watsonx.governance and CI integration are optional extensions. They must not distract from or destabilize the MVP.
Technology direction
Use the following architecture only where each component has a clear role:
IBM Bob: visible runtime developer entry point; understands the requirement/change, initiates ChangeGuard and explains findings.
watsonx Orchestrate: coordinates the agent workflow.
watsonx.ai: performs requirement interpretation, hypothesis prioritization, Jest test generation and minimal patch generation.
FastMCP server: executes deterministic Git, dependency, test, proposal-validation and approved-patch operations.
Node.js backend with SQLite: evidence store, approval control, deterministic verdict computation and UI API.
Angular: one primary Mission Control screen with optional drill-down views.
Jest: real regression execution.
Git: real diff, patch validation and reset.
Replay: clearly labelled replay of a previously verified run, used only as a fallback.
Agent design
Prefer one visible ChangeGuard workflow rather than advertising many agents.
Internally, watsonx Orchestrate may coordinate:
Scope and Impact Agent
Risk Agent
Test and Verification Agent
Remediation Agent
Supervisor
However, do not add agents merely to appear agentic.
Every consequential operation must be performed by deterministic tools. Models may reason, prioritize, explain and generate candidate code, but models must not fabricate Git results, dependency paths, test output, approval status or release verdicts.
Mandatory controls
The implementation must include:
A deterministic proof_valid rule that rejects tests that fail because of syntax errors, missing imports, compilation errors or broken test setup.
A deterministic verdict computed by the backend, not by an LLM.
A stored patch proposal validated through git apply --check.
A hash binding the human approval to the exact reviewed diff.
A single-use approval control.
No automatic application of an unreviewed patch.
Evidence captured directly from tool execution.
An honest replay mode that is visibly labelled as recorded evidence.
No unsupported ROI or “prevented production outage” claim.
Prefer that the authorization secret is passed directly from the backend to the deterministic patch tool. The LLM should not receive the raw approval secret.
Deterministic verdict rules
Use rules similar to:
BLOCKED: At least one proof_valid regression test fails, or an unresolved confirmed Critical finding exists.
NEEDS REVIEW: No executable regression has been confirmed, but the analysis found an untested downstream consumer or an unresolved High-risk hypothesis.
PASSED: The approved patch has been applied, the complete suite passes and no confirmed Critical or High finding remains.
